WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.675
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:09.002
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:16.366
Unit twelve: Art, Value and Meaning. In this unit, you will debate what gives art its value.

4
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:18.723
aesthetic judgement.

5
00:00:19.423 --> 00:00:23.860
aesthetic judgement means an evaluation of beauty or artistic quality.

6
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.996
Kant wrote extensively about aesthetic judgement.

7
00:00:27.696 --> 00:00:31.196
Your turn. Say: aesthetic judgement.

8
00:00:33.896 --> 00:00:35.210
aesthetic judgement.

9
00:00:36.217 --> 00:00:37.531
artistic merit.

10
00:00:38.231 --> 00:00:42.576
artistic merit means the quality that makes a work valuable as art.

11
00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:46.013
Critics disagreed about the film's artistic merit.

12
00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:50.271
Your turn. Say: artistic merit.

13
00:00:52.971 --> 00:00:54.216
artistic merit.

14
00:00:55.217 --> 00:00:56.543
conceptual art.

15
00:00:57.243 --> 00:01:02.377
conceptual art means art in which the idea matters more than the object.

16
00:01:02.677 --> 00:01:06.453
A banana taped to a wall became famous conceptual art.

17
00:01:07.153 --> 00:01:10.618
Your turn. Say: conceptual art.

18
00:01:13.318 --> 00:01:14.633
conceptual art.

19
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:16.595
provenance.

20
00:01:17.295 --> 00:01:21.465
provenance means the documented history of an artwork's ownership.

21
00:01:21.765 --> 00:01:24.554
The museum researched the statue's provenance.

22
00:01:25.254 --> 00:01:28.034
Your turn. Say: provenance.

23
00:01:30.734 --> 00:01:31.735
provenance.

24
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:33.519
forgery.

25
00:01:34.219 --> 00:01:37.437
forgery means a fake copy presented as genuine.

26
00:01:37.737 --> 00:01:40.318
The 'masterpiece' turned out to be a forgery.

27
00:01:41.018 --> 00:01:43.914
Your turn. Say: forgery.

28
00:01:46.614 --> 00:01:47.568
forgery.

29
00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:49.817
art historian.

30
00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:54.908
art historian means an expert who studies the history of art.

31
00:01:55.208 --> 00:01:58.682
An art historian dated the carving to the 12th century.

32
00:01:59.382 --> 00:02:02.382
Your turn. Say: art historian.

33
00:02:05.082 --> 00:02:06.397
art historian.

34
00:02:07.383 --> 00:02:08.559
iconography.

35
00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:12.930
iconography means the symbols used in art and their meanings.

36
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.808
Angkor's iconography reflects Hindu and Buddhist beliefs.

37
00:02:17.508 --> 00:02:20.730
Your turn. Say: iconography.

38
00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:24.617
iconography.

39
00:02:25.617 --> 00:02:26.653
masterwork.

40
00:02:27.353 --> 00:02:30.026
masterwork means an artist's finest work.

41
00:02:30.326 --> 00:02:33.602
The Bayon's faces are a masterwork of Khmer sculpture.

42
00:02:34.302 --> 00:02:37.349
Your turn. Say: masterwork.

43
00:02:40.049 --> 00:02:41.015
masterwork.

44
00:02:42.017 --> 00:02:44.248
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:44.548 --> 00:02:47.407
Ms. Sarah, what makes a painting worth millions?

46
00:02:48.717 --> 00:02:53.456
Economists would say rarity; critics would point to artistic merit.

47
00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:59.213
And conceptual art? A banana taped to a wall sold for thousands.

48
00:03:00.517 --> 00:03:04.745
Its defenders claim that the idea, not the object, is the art.

49
00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:10.557
According to one critic I read, it was 'a joke the market took seriously'.

50
00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:17.637
A memorable line. Provenance matters too: a forgery can fool experts for decades.

51
00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.796
Art historians studying Angkor focus on iconography.

52
00:03:24.083 --> 00:03:29.905
As they should. The Bayon remains, in the view of many scholars, a true masterwork.

53
00:03:31.217 --> 00:03:34.830
Grammar focus: Precise reporting and attribution.

54
00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.581
Precise attribution shows whose opinion you are presenting.

55
00:03:38.881 --> 00:03:43.844
According to one critic, it was a joke. Its defenders claim that the idea is the art.

56
00:03:44.144 --> 00:03:47.838
In the view of many scholars, the Bayon is a masterwork.

57
00:03:48.138 --> 00:03:54.503
At C2, always make clear whose opinion you are presenting, especially when you disagree with it.

58
00:03:55.517 --> 00:03:59.144
Now read and listen. Who Decides What Art Is Worth?.

59
00:03:59.444 --> 00:04:02.640
The value of art has always been contested.

60
00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:12.823
In the eighteenth century, Kant argued that aesthetic judgement claims universal validity, even though it is based on feeling.

61
00:04:14.117 --> 00:04:20.296
Today, the art market assigns prices that sometimes seem to bear little relation to artistic merit.

62
00:04:21.583 --> 00:04:29.132
Conceptual art has intensified this debate, as its defenders claim that ideas, not objects, carry value.

63
00:04:30.417 --> 00:04:36.352
Provenance can multiply or destroy a work's price, as cases of forgery have repeatedly shown.

64
00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:44.027
Art historians, meanwhile, interpret iconography to reveal the beliefs behind ancient works.

65
00:04:45.317 --> 00:04:53.075
In the view of many scholars, Khmer sculpture at Angkor ranks among humanity's great achievements.

66
00:04:54.383 --> 00:05:00.481
Perhaps art's deepest value lies not in its price but in its power to change how we see.

67
00:05:01.783 --> 00:05:04.331
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:04.631 --> 00:05:08.314
The documented history of an artwork's ownership is its blank.

69
00:05:08.614 --> 00:05:13.870
A, provenance. B, iconography. C, forgery.

70
00:05:17.883 --> 00:05:20.487
The answer is A: provenance.

71
00:05:21.983 --> 00:05:24.320
Which phrase attributes an opinion?

72
00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:30.467
A, In the view of many critics. B, It is obvious that. C, Everybody knows.

73
00:05:34.483 --> 00:05:37.946
The answer is A: In the view of many critics.

74
00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:42.552
blank to the museum, the statue is genuine.

75
00:05:42.852 --> 00:05:46.727
A, Following. B, According. C, Regarding.

76
00:05:50.717 --> 00:05:52.937
The answer is B: According.

77
00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.959
A fake copy presented as genuine is a blank.

78
00:05:58.259 --> 00:06:03.178
A, forgery. B, masterwork. C, conceptual art.

79
00:06:07.183 --> 00:06:09.287
The answer is A: forgery.

80
00:06:10.783 --> 00:06:15.233
According to the text, what did Kant argue about aesthetic judgement?

81
00:06:15.533 --> 00:06:21.810
A, it is purely economic. B, it claims universal validity. C, it is meaningless.

82
00:06:25.817 --> 00:06:29.651
The answer is B: it claims universal validity.

83
00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:35.973
Now it's your turn. Part one: What gives a work of art its value?

84
00:06:41.273 --> 00:06:46.512
Part two: Attribute two opinions to others, then give your own view.

85
00:06:52.812 --> 00:07:07.876
Economists argue that rarity and demand set the price, while critics contend that artistic merit is what matters. In my view, value lies in a work's capacity to change how we see the world, which is why Angkor's carvings still move visitors today.

86
00:07:08.176 --> 00:07:10.651
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:16.717
Great work today! You learned eight new words and precise reporting and attribution.

88
00:07:17.017 --> 00:07:21.237
Today's value is creativity. Use your imagination when you play.

89
00:07:21.537 --> 00:07:24.944
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:28.175
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

